C = [
("h1", "6. Overseers and Elders"),
("p", "Claim: Paul names one office of oversight in the local assembly, and he calls the man in it both an elder and a bishop. He gives the qualifications at length and says almost nothing about the office’s powers."),
("sub", "6.1 One office, two names"),
("p", "In Titus 1 the two words stand in one argument. “For this cause left I thee in Crete, that thou shouldest set in order the things that are wanting, and ordain elders in every city” (Titus 1:5). The qualifications follow: “If any be blameless, the husband of one wife, having faithful children not accused of riot or unruly” (Titus 1:6). Then comes the reason: “For a bishop must be blameless, as the steward of God” (Titus 1:7). The word “For” joins the two. The elder of verse 5 is the bishop of verse 7, and the qualifications of the one are the qualifications of the other. The King James Version’s “bishop” translates the word that means an overseer."),
("p", "Paul’s other uses agree. He writes “with the bishops and deacons” (Philippians 1:1). He calls “the elders of the church” of Ephesus (Acts 20:17) and tells them that the Holy Ghost has made them “overseers” (Acts 20:28). In 1 Timothy 5:17 he speaks of “the elders that rule well” and in 3:1–2 of “a bishop.” Thomas Constable says of the Greek word for the overseer in 1 Timothy 3:1 that it is “evidently synonymous with” the word for elder. John Piper reads the same way and writes of the elder, the overseer and the pastor that “those are the same office in Paul’s mind.” Both add the word “pastor” to the list. The volume agrees with them that elder and overseer are one, because Titus 1:5–7 says so in one argument, and it does not add “pastor,” for the reason given in chapter 5."),
("sub", "6.2 The qualifications"),
("p", "The qualifications in 1 Timothy 3 are given in a list: “A bishop then must be blameless, the husband of one wife, vigilant, sober, of good behaviour, given to hospitality, apt to teach; Not given to wine, no striker, not greedy of filthy lucre; but patient, not a brawler, not covetous; One that ruleth well his own house, having his children in subjection with all gravity” (1 Timothy 3:2–4). Paul adds the reason for the last: “(For if a man know not how to rule his own house, how shall he take care of the church of God?)” (1 Timothy 3:5). And: “Not a novice, lest being lifted up with pride he fall into the condemnation of the devil” (1 Timothy 3:6). And: “Moreover he must have a good report of them which are without” (1 Timothy 3:7)."),
("p", "The list in Titus is similar. “Not selfwilled, not soon angry,” and then “a lover of hospitality, a lover of good men, sober, just, holy, temperate; Holding fast the faithful word as he hath been taught, that he may be able by sound doctrine both to exhort and to convince the gainsayers” (Titus 1:7–9). Two things are asked of the overseer beyond character. He must be “apt to teach” (1 Timothy 3:2), and he must “rule” (1 Timothy 3:4, 5; 5:17). The volume notes that these two are the ones the passages place on the overseer and do not place in the same words on the deacon."),
("sub", "6.3 What the passages say of the office’s work"),
("p", "The work is stated briefly. “If a man desire the office of a bishop, he desireth a good work” (1 Timothy 3:1). The man is to “take care of the church of God” (1 Timothy 3:5). The elders “that rule well” are “counted worthy of double honour, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine” (1 Timothy 5:17). Those who labour in the word and doctrine are, in that verse, a group within the elders. The sentence says “especially,” and the volume reports it as written. It does not say that the elders who labour in the word are a separate class, and it does not say that all elders do."),
("p", "Thomas Constable draws a limit from the silence: “Paul did not say that each congregation of Christians required at least one elder to be a church.” The volume agrees. Paul’s letters to Timothy and Titus are about appointing elders, and they do not make an elder a condition of being a church. The word Paul uses of the Ephesian elders, “overseers,” and of the Philippian “bishops,” is in the plural each time."),
("ctbl", "What Paul asks of the overseer, and where.", [
    ("What is asked", "Where", "The kind of requirement"),
    ("blameless; the husband of one wife", "1 Timothy 3:2; Titus 1:6, 7", "Character and household"),
    ("vigilant, sober, of good behaviour, given to hospitality", "1 Timothy 3:2", "Character"),
    ("apt to teach; able to exhort and convince the gainsayers", "1 Timothy 3:2; Titus 1:9", "Teaching"),
    ("ruleth well his own house; take care of the church of God", "1 Timothy 3:4, 5", "Rule"),
    ("not a novice; a good report of them which are without", "1 Timothy 3:6, 7", "Standing"),
    ("worthy of double honour, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine", "1 Timothy 5:17", "Honour due"),
  ], [0.44, 0.28, 0.28]),
("sub", "6.4 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Elder and bishop name one office in Titus 1:5–7, and Paul’s other uses agree.",
    "The qualifications are character and household first; teaching and rule are the two works the passages name.",
    "Elders who labour in the word and doctrine are named within the elders as worthy of double honour.",
    "Paul does not make an elder a condition of a church’s being a church.",
  ]),
]
